WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:03.661
Welcome to Module 3 of the
Data Platform and SQL Server

2
00:00:03.661 --> 00:00:06.925
Identity learning path: Arc
and the engine's identity.

3
00:00:06.925 --> 00:00:08.593
It teaches Runbook R oh four,

4
00:00:08.593 --> 00:00:13.225
with Chapter 3 of the developer
workbook, Building SQL Server Identity.

5
00:00:13.225 --> 00:00:18.125
Module 2 left every current, non-clustered
instance at "Category 1 or 2".

6
00:00:18.125 --> 00:00:21.520
In this module you settle
that category from evidence.

7
00:00:25.320 --> 00:00:26.657
Why does this matter?

8
00:00:26.657 --> 00:00:30.832
An on-premises SQL Server host
has no cloud identity of its own.

9
00:00:30.832 --> 00:00:35.038
Connecting it to Azure Arc gives the
machine a system-assigned managed identity,

10
00:00:35.038 --> 00:00:39.119
and that identity is what lets the instance
take part in Entra authentication.

11
00:00:39.119 --> 00:00:41.457
No Arc, no machine identity.

12
00:00:41.457 --> 00:00:44.133
No machine identity, no Entra login.

13
00:00:44.133 --> 00:00:48.284
In this module you settle each
instance's category from evidence:

14
00:00:48.284 --> 00:00:53.587
the agent's status, the SQL extension's
state, and the instance's own Entra setting,

15
00:00:53.587 --> 00:00:56.820
read from the cloud a G
C C Moderate tenant uses.

16
00:01:00.620 --> 00:01:03.389
Here's what you'll be able to
do by the end of this module,

17
00:01:03.389 --> 00:01:05.970
which takes about fifty-five
minutes with the lab.

18
00:01:05.970 --> 00:01:10.390
You'll explain what the Arc agent's
managed identity gives the engine,

19
00:01:10.390 --> 00:01:15.645
and what the instance uses as its Entra
identity on SQL Server 2022 and on 2025.

20
00:01:15.645 --> 00:01:19.365
You'll explain why engine
Entra authentication, Layer 1,

21
00:01:19.365 --> 00:01:22.358
doesn't wait for O S level
Entra sign-in, Layer 2.

22
00:01:22.358 --> 00:01:24.437
You'll put the deployment steps in order,

23
00:01:24.437 --> 00:01:26.832
and name the checks that
gate login migration.

24
00:01:26.832 --> 00:01:30.433
And you'll settle each audited
instance's category with the kit,

25
00:01:30.433 --> 00:01:33.757
and say why it refuses a snapshot
from an unrecorded cloud.

26
00:01:33.757 --> 00:01:36.270
Before you start, finish Module 2.

27
00:01:36.270 --> 00:01:38.408
For Resource Graph and its paging,

28
00:01:38.408 --> 00:01:42.220
see Module 4 of the Microsoft
Automation learning path.

29
00:01:46.020 --> 00:01:48.332
Unit 2 is what Arc gives the engine.

30
00:01:48.332 --> 00:01:52.786
Installing the Connected Machine agent
makes the server an Azure Resource Manager

31
00:01:52.786 --> 00:01:54.974
resource, with a system-assigned managed

32
00:01:54.974 --> 00:01:57.419
identity whose credential
the agent manages.

33
00:01:57.419 --> 00:02:01.020
What the instance uses as its Entra
identity depends on the version.

34
00:02:01.020 --> 00:02:05.067
On SQL Server 2025, it's the
Arc server's managed identity,

35
00:02:05.067 --> 00:02:08.370
used as the instance's
primary managed identity.

36
00:02:08.370 --> 00:02:12.207
Microsoft Learn says only
system-assigned identities are supported.

37
00:02:12.207 --> 00:02:16.416
On SQL Server 2022, it's an app
registration with a certificate kept in

38
00:02:16.416 --> 00:02:20.483
Azure Key Vault, which the agent
downloads with the machine's identity.

39
00:02:20.483 --> 00:02:25.252
On either version, Entra authentication
needs SQL Server 2022 or later,

40
00:02:25.252 --> 00:02:29.945
is disabled by default, and isn't
supported on a failover cluster instance.

41
00:02:29.945 --> 00:02:34.245
And neither replaces the Windows
account the SQL Server service runs as.

42
00:02:38.040 --> 00:02:39.903
Two distinctions matter here.

43
00:02:39.903 --> 00:02:41.903
The first is the two layers.

44
00:02:41.903 --> 00:02:46.577
Layer 1, engine Entra authentication,
proceeds on a domain-joined host.

45
00:02:46.577 --> 00:02:50.965
Layer 2, O S level Entra sign-in,
is a separate transformation.

46
00:02:50.965 --> 00:02:55.211
So a domain-joined Windows Server
host running SQL Server 2022 with Arc

47
00:02:55.211 --> 00:02:59.115
doesn't have to leave the domain
before its engine takes Entra logins.

48
00:02:59.115 --> 00:03:00.765
The second is the cloud.

49
00:03:00.765 --> 00:03:04.769
Microsoft Learn says Office
365 G C C is paired with

50
00:03:04.769 --> 00:03:08.677
Microsoft Entra I D in
Azure, the commercial cloud.

51
00:03:08.677 --> 00:03:12.446
So a G C C Moderate tenant's Arc
resources are in a commercial

52
00:03:12.446 --> 00:03:16.753
Azure subscription in the same
tenant, the cloud called Azure Cloud.

53
00:03:16.753 --> 00:03:19.380
The program recorded that as a decision.

54
00:03:23.180 --> 00:03:25.868
Unit 3 is the sequence and the gate.

55
00:03:25.868 --> 00:03:27.617
Arc deployment runs in order.

56
00:03:27.617 --> 00:03:29.142
Connectivity first.

57
00:03:29.142 --> 00:03:30.267
Then the agent.

58
00:03:30.267 --> 00:03:32.743
Then the Azure extension for SQL Server,

59
00:03:32.743 --> 00:03:36.318
which Arc installs automatically
on a host with SQL Server.

60
00:03:36.318 --> 00:03:39.388
Then Entra authentication,
turned on for the instance,

61
00:03:39.388 --> 00:03:41.093
since it's disabled by default.

62
00:03:41.093 --> 00:03:44.454
Verification is the gate between
enrollment and migration.

63
00:03:44.454 --> 00:03:48.555
The first check asks the agent's
local identity endpoint for a token.

64
00:03:48.555 --> 00:03:52.080
The second is a test Entra login
that connects to the instance.

65
00:03:52.080 --> 00:03:56.430
And the third is the next audit run, which
should put the instance in Category 1.

66
00:03:56.430 --> 00:04:01.255
A failed verification is an enrollment
problem, solved before migration starts.

67
00:04:01.255 --> 00:04:05.684
Pre-2022 instances and failover
cluster instances upgrade,

68
00:04:05.684 --> 00:04:08.840
re-platform, or carry a dated exception.

69
00:04:12.640 --> 00:04:15.830
The kit reads three things
for each instance, the agent,

70
00:04:15.830 --> 00:04:19.728
the extension and the Entra setting,
and names whatever is missing.

71
00:04:19.728 --> 00:04:24.454
A failover cluster instance, or
a version before SQL Server 2022,

72
00:04:24.454 --> 00:04:27.353
is Category 3 before
Arc is even looked at.

73
00:04:27.353 --> 00:04:31.239
An agent that isn't connected, an
extension that didn't succeed,

74
00:04:31.239 --> 00:04:35.889
or Entra not configured makes it Category
2, with each missing piece named.

75
00:04:35.889 --> 00:04:41.002
All three, on 2022 or later, is
Category 1: plan the logins.

76
00:04:41.002 --> 00:04:44.121
It refuses any snapshot whose
cloud isn't Azure Cloud,

77
00:04:44.121 --> 00:04:47.877
because a snapshot from the wrong
cloud judges the wrong estate.

78
00:04:47.877 --> 00:04:50.744
And Resource Graph doesn't
give it the Entra setting,

79
00:04:50.744 --> 00:04:53.590
so that's confirmed in
the portal and recorded.

80
00:04:57.390 --> 00:05:01.093
Chapter 3 lists what Microsoft
Learn's material leaves to you,

81
00:05:01.093 --> 00:05:02.328
and what the kit adds.

82
00:05:02.328 --> 00:05:04.252
Each row is pinned by a test.

83
00:05:04.252 --> 00:05:09.012
The agent, the extension and the Entra
setting are three resources and one setting,

84
00:05:09.012 --> 00:05:12.512
so an instance with two of the
three looks ready in the portal;

85
00:05:12.512 --> 00:05:15.591
the kit reads all three, and
names whatever is missing.

86
00:05:15.591 --> 00:05:19.659
The category rules are in the
data platform book, not in Learn,

87
00:05:19.659 --> 00:05:24.785
so a pre-2022 instance or a cluster could
be scheduled for Arc work it can't use;

88
00:05:24.785 --> 00:05:28.203
the kit puts it in Category
3 before Arc is looked at.

89
00:05:28.203 --> 00:05:32.891
Resource Graph returns nothing, not an
error, for what the caller can't read,

90
00:05:32.891 --> 00:05:36.580
so the kit reports the agent
as not installed and keeps the

91
00:05:36.580 --> 00:05:40.115
instance in Category 2, and
Lab 2 checks the sign-in first.

92
00:05:40.115 --> 00:05:41.932
The cloud is a sign-in choice,

93
00:05:41.932 --> 00:05:45.803
so the kit refuses a snapshot
from any cloud but Azure Cloud.

94
00:05:45.803 --> 00:05:49.593
And the managed identity
method is SQL Server 2025's:

95
00:05:49.593 --> 00:05:52.960
a 2022 instance recorded
that way gets a note.

96
00:05:56.760 --> 00:05:57.647
Try it.

97
00:05:57.647 --> 00:06:01.171
Lab 1 runs in PowerShell 7 on
the kit's fictional estate,

98
00:06:01.171 --> 00:06:03.473
with no subscription and no tenant.

99
00:06:03.473 --> 00:06:06.568
Go to the kit's folder, and
make this run's results folder,

100
00:06:06.568 --> 00:06:10.834
named for the time you start, so running
the lab again never overwrites a run.

101
00:06:10.834 --> 00:06:14.164
Step one reads the fictional
Arc results: their cloud,

102
00:06:14.164 --> 00:06:17.947
Azure Cloud, and the three
machines Resource Graph returned.

103
00:06:17.947 --> 00:06:22.310
Sequel P R D oh one and sequel
P R D oh six are connected.

104
00:06:22.310 --> 00:06:25.718
The cluster node, sequel
F C I oh four dash N one,

105
00:06:25.718 --> 00:06:28.620
is disconnected, on an older agent.

106
00:06:32.420 --> 00:06:34.670
Step two settles the categories.

107
00:06:34.670 --> 00:06:39.492
Sequel P R D oh one and sequel
P R D oh six are Category 1:

108
00:06:39.492 --> 00:06:41.758
ready, so plan the logins.

109
00:06:41.758 --> 00:06:43.383
Notice the Entra column.

110
00:06:43.383 --> 00:06:48.957
Sequel P R D oh one uses an app
registration, as SQL Server 2022 does.

111
00:06:48.957 --> 00:06:53.807
Sequel P R D oh six, on 2025,
uses the managed identity.

112
00:06:53.807 --> 00:06:58.606
Sequel P R D oh two is Category 2, and
the next column names every missing piece:

113
00:06:58.606 --> 00:07:02.491
the agent isn't installed, the
SQL extension isn't deployed,

114
00:07:02.491 --> 00:07:05.233
and Entra authentication isn't configured.

115
00:07:05.233 --> 00:07:09.146
Sequel F C I oh four is
Category 3 whatever Arc shows.

116
00:07:09.146 --> 00:07:13.471
Its agent column reads "cluster: see
its nodes", because a failover cluster

117
00:07:13.471 --> 00:07:17.508
instance has no machine of its own
name, so the check doesn't guess one.

118
00:07:17.508 --> 00:07:21.390
Read its node machines in the
snapshot for the re-platform plan.

119
00:07:25.190 --> 00:07:31.504
Step three opens the manifest: the arc
step, read-only, with its three controls,

120
00:07:31.504 --> 00:07:36.415
C M eight, C M six and I A five, its
three K S Is, and each file's hash.

121
00:07:36.415 --> 00:07:39.615
Step four tries a snapshot
whose cloud isn't recorded.

122
00:07:39.615 --> 00:07:43.200
It copies the fixture, sets its
environment to not recorded,

123
00:07:43.200 --> 00:07:44.590
and runs the check on it.

124
00:07:44.590 --> 00:07:49.046
The check throws: the Arc results came
from a cloud that isn't recorded,

125
00:07:49.046 --> 00:07:52.877
and a G C C Moderate tenant's
Arc resources are in Azure Cloud.

126
00:07:52.877 --> 00:07:54.791
Then Test-Path shows false.

127
00:07:54.791 --> 00:07:57.960
The check refused the
snapshot and wrote nothing.

128
00:08:01.760 --> 00:08:06.135
Step five leaves sequel P R D oh
one's Entra setting unconfirmed.

129
00:08:06.135 --> 00:08:10.085
This is what every instance looks
like in Lab 2, until you confirm it.

130
00:08:10.085 --> 00:08:13.100
The agent is connected and
the extension is deployed,

131
00:08:13.100 --> 00:08:17.212
but with the Entra setting unconfirmed,
the instance drops to Category 2,

132
00:08:17.212 --> 00:08:21.872
and the next column names the missing
piece: Entra authentication not configured.

133
00:08:21.872 --> 00:08:24.986
An instance with two of the
three looks ready in the portal;

134
00:08:24.986 --> 00:08:26.672
the kit doesn't treat it as ready.

135
00:08:26.672 --> 00:08:30.734
Step six runs this chapter's
tests: five pass, none fail.

136
00:08:30.734 --> 00:08:32.535
Step seven lists your runs.

137
00:08:32.535 --> 00:08:36.450
There's nothing to clean up: the
results stay in the run's own folder.

138
00:08:40.250 --> 00:08:42.925
Lab 2 runs on your own test subscription.

139
00:08:42.925 --> 00:08:44.900
It hasn't been run by the authors.

140
00:08:44.900 --> 00:08:48.661
You need a commercial Azure test
subscription in the test tenant,

141
00:08:48.661 --> 00:08:51.864
which is where a G C C Moderate
tenant's Arc resources live,

142
00:08:51.864 --> 00:08:56.600
with at least one Arc-enabled test server
running SQL Server, never production.

143
00:08:56.600 --> 00:09:01.119
You need PowerShell 7 with the Az
Accounts and Az Resource Graph modules,

144
00:09:01.119 --> 00:09:05.036
a person who holds Reader on the
subscription and nothing more,

145
00:09:05.036 --> 00:09:08.275
and Module 2's Lab 2 snapshot
from the same instances.

146
00:09:08.275 --> 00:09:12.814
Sign in and confirm the cloud, export
an Arc snapshot through Resource Graph,

147
00:09:12.814 --> 00:09:16.200
and settle the categories as
Resource Graph leaves them.

148
00:09:16.200 --> 00:09:19.381
Then confirm one instance's
Entra setting in the portal,

149
00:09:19.381 --> 00:09:22.838
record it in a separate copy,
and settle the categories again.

150
00:09:22.838 --> 00:09:24.563
Nothing in Azure changes.

151
00:09:24.563 --> 00:09:28.090
To back it out, sign out
and delete the lab's files.

152
00:09:31.890 --> 00:09:33.490
The federal requirement.

153
00:09:33.490 --> 00:09:39.340
Chapter 3's table maps the check to
three controls in NIST SP 800-53.

154
00:09:39.340 --> 00:09:44.104
Recording each instance's agent, extension
and host machine supports C M eight,

155
00:09:44.104 --> 00:09:45.863
System Component Inventory,

156
00:09:45.863 --> 00:09:49.528
with the snapshot as exported
and the run's files as evidence.

157
00:09:49.528 --> 00:09:52.447
Checking each instance's
Entra configuration,

158
00:09:52.447 --> 00:09:56.039
and refusing results from the
wrong cloud, supports C M six,

159
00:09:56.039 --> 00:10:00.903
Configuration Settings; keep the confirmed
snapshot, naming who confirmed it.

160
00:10:00.903 --> 00:10:04.241
And naming which instances can
take Entra logins without a

161
00:10:04.241 --> 00:10:07.715
stored password supports I A
five, Authenticator Management.

162
00:10:07.715 --> 00:10:12.277
Runbook R oh four lists the evidence
for the deployment itself, ring by ring.

163
00:10:12.277 --> 00:10:16.902
K S Is are FedRAMP twenty X indicators
for cloud service providers.

164
00:10:16.902 --> 00:10:19.917
The run's files provide
evidence for each control;

165
00:10:19.917 --> 00:10:24.050
the agency still sets its
parameters, and the assessor decides.

166
00:10:27.850 --> 00:10:29.900
Check yourself with two questions.

167
00:10:29.900 --> 00:10:34.150
First: what does the check do with a snapshot
whose environment isn't Azure Cloud?

168
00:10:34.150 --> 00:10:37.862
The answer is a: it refuses
it, and writes nothing.

169
00:10:37.862 --> 00:10:43.263
Second: on SQL Server 2022, what does
the instance use as its Entra identity?

170
00:10:43.263 --> 00:10:47.680
The answer is c: an app registration
with a certificate in Azure Key Vault,

171
00:10:47.680 --> 00:10:50.037
downloaded to the host by the Arc agent.

172
00:10:50.037 --> 00:10:53.888
The managed identity method
is SQL Server 2025's.

173
00:10:53.888 --> 00:10:58.530
The module page has two more questions,
each linked to the section that answers it.

174
00:11:02.330 --> 00:11:03.355
To sum up.

175
00:11:03.355 --> 00:11:08.136
You learned what Arc gives the engine,
how its Entra identity differs between

176
00:11:08.136 --> 00:11:13.268
SQL Server 2022 and 2025, and the sequence
and checks that gate login migration.

177
00:11:13.268 --> 00:11:16.297
You settled four instances'
categories from evidence,

178
00:11:16.297 --> 00:11:19.530
and saw the check refuse results
from an unrecorded cloud.

179
00:11:19.530 --> 00:11:24.335
The evidence an I S S O keeps from this
module is the Arc snapshot as Resource Graph

180
00:11:24.335 --> 00:11:28.781
returned it, the run's files with their
manifest, and the confirmed snapshot,

181
00:11:28.781 --> 00:11:33.442
naming who confirmed the Entra setting
and when, with the second run's manifest.

182
00:11:33.442 --> 00:11:36.455
Before shipping, work through
Chapter 3's checklist.

183
